.TH CYAML_DICTIONARY_FIRST 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_dictionary_first \- start an iteration over the members of a dictionary node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

typedef struct cyaml_dictionary_iter {
    const char *key;
    cyaml       value;
    /* private fields, whose names begin with an underscore */
} cyaml_dictionary_iter;

bool cyaml_dictionary_first(cyaml dict, cyaml_dictionary_iter *it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Fills the cursor it and steps it onto the first member of the
dictionary node dict.  cyaml_dictionary_next(3) then steps it onto
each later member.  After a call that returns true, it->key is the key
of the member and it->value is its value.  it->value is a borrowed
reference, with the rules of cyaml_dictionary_get(3).

The members come in insertion order: the order in which each key
first entered the dictionary.  For a parsed document that is the
order of the members in the text.  For a dictionary that the program
builds, it is the order of the cyaml_dictionary_set(3) and cyaml_set(3)
calls that added each key.  A replacement of the value of a key keeps
its place, so a key that a document repeats keeps the place of its
first occurrence and the value of its last.  A key that the program
removes and then sets again goes after every other member.

The members that a merge key ("<<") brings in take the place of the
"<<" entry: after the explicit members before it and before the
explicit members after it, source by source in the order that the
entry lists them, each source in its own member order.  A key that the
mapping already holds keeps its own place and value.

cyaml_serialize(3), cyaml_serialize_flow(3) and cyaml_clone(3) use the
same order, so a parse and then a serialize keeps the key order of the
source document.

The cursor is a plain struct that the caller owns, usually on its own
stack.  The iteration allocates nothing and cannot fail.  There is
nothing to release, and the caller can stop at any point.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dict  The dictionary node to walk.
it    The cursor to fill.  It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true when it now stands on the first member.

false when dict has no members, when dict is NULL, or when dict is
not a CYAML_DICTIONARY.  The function then sets it->key and
it->value to NULL.  false also when it is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
it->key points at the copy of the key that the dictionary stores.  It
stays valid until that member is removed or the dictionary is
destroyed.

The cursor holds the member that the next step reads, which is the
successor of the member that the last successful call gave.  It stays
valid across every change to the dictionary that leaves that successor
in place:

  - a replacement of the value of any member, the current one
    included, through cyaml_dictionary_set(3) or cyaml_set(3).  The
    member keeps its place, so the iteration goes on in the same
    order;
  - the removal of the current member, or of any member other than
    the successor.  To remove the current member, pass it->key to
    cyaml_dictionary_remove(3);
  - an insert of a new key.  The new member goes after every other
    member.  Whether this iteration still reaches it is unspecified.

A removal of the successor, through cyaml_dictionary_remove(3),
cyaml_delete(3) or any other call, invalidates the cursor, and so does
a destroy of the dictionary.  A later cyaml_dictionary_next(3) on an
invalid cursor is undefined behavior.  Start again with
cyaml_dictionary_first() after such a change.

it->key and it->value describe the current member as it was when the
cursor stepped onto it.  A replacement of the value of the current
member through cyaml_dictionary_set(3) destroys the old value, so
it->value then names freed memory; read the new value with
cyaml_dictionary_get(3) and it->key.  cyaml_set(3) updates the
existing node in place, so it->value stays the same node.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
A cyaml tree is not thread safe.  Several threads can walk the same
dictionary at the same time, each with its own cursor, while no thread
changes the dictionary.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml doc = cyaml_parse("b: 1\ena: 2\en", NULL);
cyaml_dictionary_iter it;
for (bool ok = cyaml_dictionary_first(doc, &it); ok;
     ok = cyaml_dictionary_next(&it)) {
    printf("%s = %lld\en", it.key, cyaml_int_val(it.value));
}
/* prints "b = 1" and then "a = 2" */

/* Remove every member whose value is null, in one pass. */
for (bool ok = cyaml_dictionary_first(doc, &it); ok;
     ok = cyaml_dictionary_next(&it)) {
    if (cyaml_type(it.value) == CYAML_NULL)
        cyaml_dictionary_remove(doc, it.key);
}
cyaml_destroy(doc);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_dictionary_next (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_size (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize (3)
